# Default: let Git normalise text so the repo stays LF-canonical regardless of
# which platform a contributor is on.
* text=auto

# ── Captured wire recordings ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# These are evidence, not source. They are byte-for-byte records of what a
# Harness actually put on a pipe, and several findings turn on the exact
# framing — ACP and Pi RPC are both newline-delimited JSON, so the line
# terminator IS part of the protocol under test.
#
# '-text' disables all end-of-line conversion in both directions: the bytes
# checked in are the bytes checked out, on every platform. Note these files
# are currently CRLF because that is genuinely what the Harnesses emitted on
# Windows; that is data worth preserving, not a defect to normalise away.
docs/research/captures/** -text

# ── Executables ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Run under bash (Git Bash on Windows, sh on Linux/WSL) and by python3 on
# Linux. A CRLF shebang line makes a POSIX kernel look for an interpreter
# named "bash\r", so these must stay LF wherever they are checked out.
*.sh text eol=lf
*.py text eol=lf

# ── Go ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# gofmt emits LF; keep it that way so diffs stay clean.
*.go text eol=lf
go.mod text eol=lf
go.sum text eol=lf
